# The on-prem bundle's configuration. Copy to `.env` and fill in.
#
#     cp .env.example .env
#     docker compose up -d --build
#
# Compose refuses to start if any of the four required values below is missing,
# rather than booting a stack with an open gateway or an unidentifiable install.

# ── Required ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The bearer the console, console-v2 and admin present to the gateway. It is
# ALSO what they are given as their "vendor key" — they hold no real Anthropic or
# Interfaze credential, so a compromised console cannot spend the model budget
# or send anything that skips the audit.
#   openssl rand -hex 32
GATEWAY_TOKEN=

# Shared secret between console-v2 and admin for ticket threads and attachment
# previews (apps/console-v2/lib/attachments.ts). Must match on both, which the
# compose file arranges. Unset = previews fail closed.
#   openssl rand -hex 32
INTERNAL_TOKEN=

# Who this install is, in the fleet. An opaque handle, not a customer name —
# it appears in every metrics row we receive.
METRICS_INSTALL_ID=

# Key-encryption key for employee documents in the portal. The documents are
# AES-256-GCM sealed under a per-file key wrapped by this one. **Losing it means
# losing every stored ID card and payslip** — back it up outside this machine.
#   openssl rand -base64 32
PORTAL_FILE_KEK=

# The ת"ז keyring (PAY-112) — `<version>:<base64url of 64 random bytes>`, comma
# separated. The first 32 bytes are the blind-index pepper, the last 32 the
# AES key. Must NOT live where the database lives: a dump plus this value
# recovers every identity number, and a dump without it recovers none.
#
# **Required by every app in the bundle**, not just the portal — the console
# lists employees and the first one it touches throws without it.
#
#   node -e "console.log('v1:'+require('crypto').randomBytes(64).toString('base64url'))"
NATIONAL_ID_KEYS=
# Which version new writes use. Blank = the last one listed. Only ever changed
# as step 2 of the four-step rotation in packages/core/src/national-id.ts.
NATIONAL_ID_ACTIVE_KEY=

# Bearer for the Michpal connector. Only needed with `--profile michpal`.
#   openssl rand -hex 32
WORKER_TOKEN=

# What the scheduler presents to the scheduled routes. Must match what the
# apps expect (they check `Authorization: Bearer $CRON_SECRET`).
#
# **This one is load-bearing.** The portal's job queue is durable but passive —
# it drains only when `/api/jobs/run` is called. In the hosted deployment that
# caller is Vercel Cron; on-prem it is the `scheduler` container. Get this wrong
# and reminders stop being sent with every health check still green.
#   openssl rand -hex 32
CRON_SECRET=

# Which scheduled jobs run. Blank = the sensible on-prem set. `all` adds the
# optional ones; or name them: portal.jobs,admin.monitor,console.policyVerify
SCHEDULER_JOBS=

# Which kind of install this is. `onprem` makes every storage decision local and
# treats the gateway as mandatory; `cloud` is the hosted default. Set once —
# the individual settings derive from it (packages/core/src/deploy-target.ts).
DEPLOY_TARGET=onprem

# ── The model vendors ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# These live ONLY in the gateway container. Leave one blank to disable that
# vendor: calls to it then refuse with NOT_CONFIGURED naming the variable,
# which is the supported way to run an install that talks to one vendor only.
#
# docs/on-prem-architecture.md §6.2 is the argument for a third option — a key
# on the CUSTOMER's own Anthropic or Bedrock account. Nothing here prevents
# that; it is their key in their file, and their data agreement rather than ours.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
INTERFAZE_API_KEY=

# ── Gateway policy ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# enforce  pseudonymize before sending. The default and the only setting that
#          protects anything.
# report   pseudonymize, record what WOULD have been hidden, and forward the
#          original. For the first week of an install, to see the numbers before
#          changing behaviour. It does not protect traffic and says so on boot.
# off      no pseudonymization at all.
GATEWAY_MODE=enforce

# What is hidden. `money` is available and deliberately not on by default:
# document extraction has to read amounts, and an amount with no name attached
# to it is not identifying. See packages/core/src/pseudonymize.ts.
#   id · email · phone · person · org · money
GATEWAY_KINDS=email,id,phone,person,org

# What to do with a request carrying a whole document (a payslip PDF, a scanned
# ת.ז.). A text pseudonymizer cannot read into base64, so those bytes would
# cross the boundary intact.
#
# block  refuse the request with a readable Hebrew error. The default.
# allow  forward the document whole. A real choice a bureau can make — but make
#        it knowingly: this is the one setting that sends unredacted payroll
#        documents to a third party.
GATEWAY_ATTACHMENTS=block

# ── Metrics ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Where the rollup is pushed. Leave BLANK for an install that reports nothing —
# a valid configuration; /metrics still serves locally.
METRICS_CONTROL_PLANE_URL=
METRICS_INSTALL_TOKEN=

# ── The fleet dashboard (Better Stack) ────────────────────────────────────────
#
# One source holds both fleets. The hosted deployment pushes the same rollup,
# built by the same function, tagged `cloud` instead of `onprem` — so every
# question that is really a comparison ("is the gateway slower on customer
# hardware?") is one query rather than two.
#
# It ships the SAME payload the control-plane push sends, through the SAME
# guard: counts, codes, hashes and timestamps, and nothing that has the shape of
# free text. `GET /snapshot` on this service shows the operator exactly what
# leaves. Leave both blank for an install that reports nothing.
#
#   Better Stack → Telemetry → Sources → your source → "Ingesting host"
BETTERSTACK_INGEST_HOST=
BETTERSTACK_SOURCE_TOKEN=

# A Better Stack Uptime heartbeat, pinged only after a rollup that got out.
#
# This is the half telemetry cannot do: an install that dies sends no events,
# and no events looks exactly like a quiet week. With a heartbeat, silence is
# what alerts.
BETTERSTACK_HEARTBEAT_URL=

# Salt for hashing client ids before they are reported, so the control plane
# cannot join two bureaus' clients. If unset, a random one is generated at every
# boot — safe, but per-client series reset on each restart.
#   openssl rand -hex 32
METRICS_HASH_SALT=

# ── Ports and storage ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

MONGODB_DB=payroll
CONSOLE_V2_PORT=3201
CONSOLE_PORT=3200
ADMIN_PORT=3300

# Where Mongo can write dumps for the bureau's own backup regime to collect.
BACKUP_DIR=./backups

# The image tag. Pin it on a real install — docs/on-prem-architecture.md §6.4:
# `:latest` on a box you cannot inspect is how you get an outage you cannot
# reproduce.
BUNDLE_VERSION=dev
